it doesn't get more succinct than a mere  hello  that was u.s president joe biden's  response when asked if he had a message  for north korean leader kim jong-un but  he offered a lengthier answer when asked  how the u.s and south korea would deal  with nuclear or missile threats  we are prepared for anything north korea  does we've had we fought through how we  respond to whatever they do and so i'm  not concerned if that's what you're  suggesting  just before he left for tokyo to  continue his tour of asia biden and  south korean president yun sukyo visited  an air force command center  the air and space operation center here  is a key place for the republic of korea  and the united states to jointly respond  to the increasingly sophisticated north  korean nuclear and missile threats and  it is a symbol of the republic of korea  and u.s alliance  the joint appearance the choice of  location to showcase the country's  strong security alliance  a day earlier the two leaders had agreed  to step up military exercises and  possibly deploy more strategic weapons  to the region to counter north korean  threats  there was also an offer to return to  negotiations although some analysts say  north korea is unlikely to respond just  yet i think they're waiting to gauge  uh what is going on with the new  president of south korea uh president  yun sukyo  and uh they already know a lot about  biden from his past dealings uh in the  u.s senate and as vice president  so i think they're just going to sort of  bide their time and see what cooperative  efforts may be in the future  north korea is dealing with a severe  outbreak of covet 19 and has not  responded to international offers of  assistance including from the u.s  but biden's visit was as much about  security as it was about the economy and  he used it to highlight multi-billion  dollar investments south korean  companies were making in the us  biden will be in japan till tuesday  where he's expected to continue with the  